[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m signal 13), Rust returned `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library installs `SIG_IGN` for `SIGPIPE` before
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` runs. The write to the dead pipe therefore returned `EPIPE` instead of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m killing the process; `cstdio::write_through` discards write errors (which is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m correct — glibc's `exit` ignores them too), so the program ran to a clean `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The C program keeps the default disposition and is killed by the signal.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Every stdout byte in this program is written by a single flush at exit, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m visible output was identical; only the exit status differed. A test that checked
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout alone would have passed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `translation/src/main.rs` (and `src/bin/probe.rs`) restore the default
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m disposition as the first statement of `main`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fn restore_default_sigpipe() {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     const SIGPIPE: i32 = 13;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     const SIG_DFL: usize = 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     extern "C" { fn signal(signum: i32, handler: usize) -> usize; }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     unsafe { signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL); }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `sigpipe_exit_status_matches` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `sigpipe_signal_matches_without_a_shell`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 2 — the C reference built with `NDEBUG` (measurement error, FIXED)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** On the first comparison the C program printed `(empty tree)` where
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust printed a ten-node tree, and produced *no* stderr where Rust produced two
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m error lines. Ten stdout lines and both stderr lines "differed".
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** Not the translation. I had configured the C build with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release`, which adds `-DNDEBUG`. `assert(...)` then
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m expands to nothing — and `main.c` performs nearly all of its work *inside*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m asserts:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m assert(tree_add_node(tree, 1, 0, "root") == 0);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m With `NDEBUG` those calls are deleted outright. No tree is ever built, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tree_print` reports `(empty tree)` and no duplicate-id or max-children error is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ever provoked. The C binary was a hollow shell and the comparison measured
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m nothing.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** Build the C side exactly as the task specifies — `cmake ..` with no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m build type, which leaves `C_FLAGS` empty and asserts live. The test suite
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m compiles the C sources itself with `cc -std=c11` and no `-DNDEBUG`, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `driver_emits_the_expected_error_lines` pins the trap: it asserts the C stderr is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exactly the two expected lines and that stdout contains `[10] ggc1`. An `NDEBUG`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m build fails that test instead of passing vacuously.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 3 — unspecified `printf` argument order in the probe (harness defect, FIXED)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** The `deep_recursion` scenario differed on one line:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C:    remove(1)=0 size=2499 has_root=1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: remove(1)=0 size=0    has_root=0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** My probe, not the translation. The C probe had written:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m printf("remove(1)=%d size=%zu has_root=%d\n",
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m        tree_remove_node(tree, 1), tree_size(tree), tree->has_root);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C does not specify the evaluation order of function arguments; gcc on x86-64
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m evaluates right-to-left, so `tree_size()` and `has_root` were read *before* the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m removal ran. Rust's argument evaluation is defined left-to-right, so it reported
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the state *after* the removal. Both programs were behaving correctly; the test
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m was asking an unanswerable question.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** Sequence the mutating call into a local before formatting, in both
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m probes. The three `hashmap_remove` calls that shared one `printf` were sequenced
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m too — their result happened to be order-independent, but the fragility was the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m same.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Input classes enumerated and verified identical
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each name is a `probe` scenario; all are compared on stdout, stderr and exit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m status, with the streams captured separately, merged into one file, and merged
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m onto one pipe.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Scenario | C branch reached |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `empty_print` | `tree_print` with `has_root == 0` → `(empty tree)`; queries on an empty tree |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `null_data` | `tree_add_node` with `data == NULL` → `node->data[0] = '\0'`; `printf("[%lu] %s\n", ...)` with an empty string, which leaves a trailing space |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `parent_missing` | `Error: Parent node %lu not found` (never reached by `main.c`) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `remove_missing` | `Error: Node %lu not found` (never reached by `main.c`), on an empty tree and on a populated one |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `queries_missing` | the `-1` returns of `tree_get_depth`, `tree_get_height`, `tree_count_descendants`, `tree_find_path` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `find_path_clamp` | `if (length > max_length) length = max_length;` — the clamp happens *after* the path is built, so `max_length=3` on a 5-deep chain returns `[3,4,5]`, not `[1,2,3]`. Also `max_length` of 0 and −1 (which returns −1 and writes nothing) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `find_path_deep` | the `while (length < 1000)` bound: a 1200-deep chain stops before reaching the root |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `data_trunc` | `strncpy(node->data, data, MAX_DATA_LENGTH - 1)` at 254, 255 and 300 bytes, the empty string, and non-UTF-8 bytes plus `%s`/`%d`/`%%` in the data |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `hashmap_reuse` | the tombstone-reuse arm of `hashmap_put`, the update-existing arm, and `hashmap_remove` of an absent key |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `hashmap_null_value` | a stored `NULL` value: the slot is occupied, yet `hashmap_contains` reports 0 and `hashmap_remove` returns `NULL` while still decrementing `size` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `hashmap_clear` | `hashmap_clear`, which `main.c` never calls, plus reuse afterwards |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `hashmap_resize` | repeated `hashmap_resize` across 300 keys with interleaved removals, so `deleted_count` contributes to the load factor and is reset by the rehash. The full slot table is dumped, which pins the FNV-1a hash, the byte order of the key, and the linear-probing order |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `big_ids` | `%lu` at `UINT64_MAX`, at `2^63`, and at 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `zero_root` | `root_id == 0`, indistinguishable from "no parent" |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `remove_root_readd` | `has_root`/`root_id` reset after removing the root; the next add becomes root and has its requested parent silently replaced by 0; previously removed ids re-added over tombstones |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `child_shift` | the `child_ids` shifting loop in `tree_remove_node`, removing first, middle and last child, then refilling |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `max_children` | `Error: Parent has maximum children` at the `MAX_CHILDREN` boundary, and that freeing one slot admits exactly one more |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `subtree_removal` | recursive `tree_remove_subtree` over a wide, deep tree |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `dup_and_reinsert` | `Error: Node with ID %lu already exists` at the root and deeper |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `interleaved` | stdout/stderr ordering around an explicit `fflush(stdout)` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `deep_recursion` | `tree_get_height`, `tree_count_descendants` and `tree_remove_subtree` recursing 5000 deep |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Process-level classes, all verified identical: no arguments and extra arguments
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (both ignored, as `main(void)` takes none); stdin left unread; streams separate,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m merged to a file, and merged to a pipe; stdout on a pipe with no reader; stdout
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m closed (`>&-`, `EBADF`); stdout on `/dev/full` (`ENOSPC`, status stays 0); a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m controlling terminal, where glibc switches stdout to line buffering (checked with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `script -qec`, outside `cargo test` because it needs util-linux); `LC_ALL` set to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `C`, `C.UTF-8`, `en_US.UTF-8`, `tr_TR.UTF-8`, `de_DE.UTF-8`; and repeated runs,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m which must be bit-identical.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Buffering, which the merged-stream cases depend on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m glibc chooses stdout's mode on first use: line-buffered on a terminal, otherwise
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m block-buffered, while stderr is unbuffered. With `2>&1` the error lines therefore
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m appear *ahead* of stdout text that was printed before them. `src/cstdio.rs`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reproduces this with its own 4096-byte buffer, an `is_terminal()` check, and a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m flush at exit; `println!` would have been line-buffered always and would have
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reordered the streams. This was already correct in the translation — no mismatch
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m was found here, but it is what the `*_with_merged_streams` tests exist to hold in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m place, and `hashmap_resize` (11 KB of stdout) exercises the buffer-full flush
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m path rather than only the flush at exit.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    187[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    188[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    189[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Branches that cannot be reached from either program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    190[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    191[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Recorded so the next reader does not look for tests that cannot exist.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    192[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    193[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Allocation-failure paths.** `hashmap_create`, `hashmap_resize`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    194[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `tree_create` and `tree_add_node` all have `if (!p) return ...` arms for a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    195[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   failed `malloc`/`calloc`. The Rust side uses infallible allocation (`Vec`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    196[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   owned values), where the equivalent condition aborts. Unreachable without an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    197[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   allocator fault injector; the surrounding success paths are covered.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    198[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`NULL` receiver guards.** `hashmap_put/get/remove/size/clear`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    199[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `hashmap_destroy`, `tree_delete`, `tree_add_node`, `tree_remove_node`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    200[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `tree_get_node` and `tree_find_path` each begin with a `!map` / `!tree` /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    201[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `!path` check. No caller in either program passes `NULL`, and the Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    202[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   translation takes `&self`/`&mut self`, which cannot be null. Structurally
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    203[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   absent rather than untested.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    204[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`hashmap_put` returning −1 for a full table.** Guarded by the resize on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    205[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   entry; the comment in `hashmap.c` says as much. Reaching it needs a failed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    206[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   resize, i.e. the allocation-failure case above. `Error: Failed to add node to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    207[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   hashmap` in `tree_add_node` is downstream of it and equally unreachable.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    208[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`tree_remove_subtree` returning −1.** Requires a `child_ids` entry naming a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    209[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   node that is not in the map. Every id in `child_ids` is inserted by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    210[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `tree_add_node` and removed only by the same recursion, so the list cannot go
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    211[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   stale while it is walked.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    212[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`tree_get_depth` returning −1 from inside its loop.** Requires a node whose
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    213[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   ancestor chain is broken before it reaches `root_id`. Removals always take the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    214[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   whole subtree, so no node can outlive its parent.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    215[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Stack exhaustion.** `tree_get_height`, `tree_count_descendants` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    216[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `tree_remove_subtree` recurse once per level. Verified equal at 5000 levels.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    217[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Deep enough to overflow, the two would differ in kind — C faults, Rust prints
